## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constrained path DP with an extra state (turns). Encourages explicit DP state design, directional transitions, and plan for path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with 4D state (i, j, t, dir)
- Transition logic depends on last direction
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Edge cases when k is large vs. maximum turns (n+m-2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>
    Array.from({length:m}, ()=>
      Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(2).fill(NEG))))
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
        for(let dir=0;dir<2;dir++){
          const cur = dp[i][j][t][dir];
          if(cur===NEG) continue;
          // move right
          if(j+1<m){
            const nt = t + (dir===0?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i][j+1][nt][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][nt][0], cur + grid[i][j+1]);
          }
          // move down
          if(i+1<n){
            const nt = t + (dir===1?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i+1][j][nt][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][nt][1], cur + grid[i+1][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = NEG;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
    ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1]);
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(n*m*k)?
- How does the solution change if k is unconstrained or if a minimum number of turns is required?

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests comprehensive DP design for partitioning problems with nonlinear segment costs involving both min and sum operations, requiring careful handling of base cases and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-dimensional DP over partition indices
- Range minimum query optimization within transitions
- Complete solution from base cases through reconstruction
- Space-time tradeoffs and optimization strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Initialize DP table and parent pointers
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  dp[i][1] = rangeMin(1, i) * prefix[i];
  parent[i][1] = 0;
}

// Fill DP table
for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
  for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
      const segVal = rangeMin(p+1, i) * (prefix[i] - prefix[p]);
      const total = dp[p][t-1] + segVal;
      if (total > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = total;
        parent[i][t] = p;
      }
    }
  }
}

// Reconstruct segments
let i = n, t = k;
while (t > 0) {
  const p = parent[i][t];
  segments[t] = [p+1, i];
  i = p;
  t--;
}
```

