- How would handle negative numbers and at-most-k segments?
- Can you optimize to O(nk log n) using monotone queues?
- What modifications needed for weighted segment costs?

## Why This Is Asked
Simulates real-world budget allocation: maximize revenue from non-overlapping campaigns with a fixed cost. Tests a DP over prefixes with a per-segment penalty and reconstruction need.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and campaign count
- Inner-max optimization to O(nk)
- Path reconstruction for window endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(daily, k, C){
  const n = daily.length; const P=[0];
  for(let x of daily) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const best=Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t]=-Infinity;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      best[t]=Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1]-P[i-1]);
      dp[i][t]=Math.max(dp[i][t], best[t]+P[i]-C);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle varying campaign costs per campaign or non-overlap gaps?
- How would you extend to maximize number of campaigns given a target revenue?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a non-adjacent selection constraint and exact count, forcing clarity on state definition and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on two dimensions (i, t) with take/skip choices. 
- Non-adjacency enforced by using i-2 when taking i. 
- Backtracking recovers chosen indices; space optimization possible.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonAdjacent(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      const val = (i>=2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t===1 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i-1];
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = true; }
    }
  }
  let res = [], i=n, t=k;
  while(i>0 && t>0){
    if(take[i][t]){ res.push(i); i-=2; t--; } else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], indices: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most k selections? 
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked
