- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming over non-overlapping subarrays where block value depends on internal structure, requiring precomputation and reconstruction techniques.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping k blocks in increasing order
- Precomputation of per-block value (peaks inside)
- DP with inner maximization over l
- Reconstruction pointers; time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k, F, P) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const peaks = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Precompute peaks
  for (let i = 1; i < n-1; i++) {
    if (A[i-1] < A[i] && A[i] > A[i+1]) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        for (let r = i; r < n; r++) {
          peaks[l][r]++;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const val = (l, r) => pref[r+1] - pref[l] - F - P * peaks[l][r];

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1) > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1);
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct
  const blocks = [];
  let cur = n, curT = k;
  while (curT > 0 && parent[cur][curT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[cur][curT];
    blocks.unshift([start, cur-1]);
    cur = start;
    curT--;
  }

  return { max: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity

- Time: O(n²k) due to nested loops for peaks, DP, and reconstruction
- Space: O(nk) for DP table and parent pointers, reducible to O(n) with rolling arrays

The key challenge is efficiently computing block values while maintaining optimal substructure across k non-overlapping segments.

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic programming on partitioning a string into numeric substrings, including numeric parsing and reconstruction, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on string partitions with exact k blocks
- Efficient substring value computation and leading-zero handling
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  // val[i][j] = value of s[i..j-1]
  const val = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (s[i] === '0') { val[i][i+1] = 0; continue; }
    let cur = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      cur = cur*10 + (s.charCodeAt(j) - 48);
      val[i][j+1] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = val[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        if (s[j] === '0' && i - j > 1) continue;
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val[j][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.push(0);
  cuts.reverse();
  return { sum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

